// Multi-TU template instances keep their own bodies at link (--project).
//
// A template instance is emitted linkonce: a multi-TU link keeps the first
// same-named copy, on the premise that copies sharing a name are one
// specialization. madc named a member template's instance (`<placeholder>
// __mti`, then `__mti__oN`) and a member constructor template's instance
// (`Class__Class`, then `__oN`) by the ORDER the TU minted them, so two TUs
// instantiating different specializations first gave them one name, and the
// link ran the first TU's body in the second: S(double) built S(int) (v=4),
// Box::count(int, double, char) answered 1, and std::map's lvalue-key
// operator[] reached the rvalue overload's _M_emplace_hint_unique
// instance and moved the key out of the vector.
//
// The build is the three .cpp TUs named in testprojectmtiorder.cc.json (the
// --project flag in the .flags fixture); this file is an ignored positional.
// The .flags fixture also says --no-forest-bind: a packed binary binding the
// forest for <string> in two or more --project TUs crashes in
// madc_thaw_member_template (B153, filed; v0.100.0 crashes alike), and this
// test is about instance naming, which the live parse exercises.
// Oracle: g++ 13 and clang++ 18 build the same three TUs and print the
// .expect lines: a = 4*100 + 1*10 + 1, b = 8*1000 + 3*100 + 3*10 + 5.
